# Every checkout gets the bytes that were committed: LF, on every OS.
#
# The knowledge map (docs/repo-map.json) records a SHA-256 of every tracked
# file, and tests/test_repo_map.py fails when a file no longer matches. A
# clone whose Git rewrote line endings on checkout (core.autocrlf=true, the
# default on a Windows runner) would make every file "changed" and the map
# stale on one OS while current on another. Pinning LF here keeps the hash
# the same everywhere, which is what lets CI run the same check on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except a batch file, which checks out CRLF (decision 209, R7). cmd reads a
# .bat by byte offset and loses its place in an LF file - Setup.bat printed a
# stray "'mputer' is not recognized" after a pause on the office computer.
# Later lines win, so this overrides the rule above for .bat alone; the
# repository still holds LF, so the map's hashes do not change.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Rendered by the tools from docs/repo-map.json and the coverage inputs and
# committed so a fresh clone reads them (CLAUDE.md's first instruction is to
# read the map); collapsed in pull-request review because nobody edits them.
docs/repo-map.md linguist-generated=true
docs/repo-map.json linguist-generated=true
docs/vocab-coverage.md linguist-generated=true
docs/vocab-coverage.json linguist-generated=true
